// EXPECTED: prints 1. That is the defect.
//
// A `&Seq<T>` PARAMETER does not deliver the Seq. The callee reads through the
// wrong indirection level and sees the address of the handle rather than the
// handle, so `.len()` and `.get(i)` return address-like garbage; a loop over
// the two together segfaults. `souc check` accepts the program -- this is
// wrong code, not a rejection.
//
// Measured 2026-09-06, Madaros built from 39d72a37a9 (the merge of #2413):
//   len through &Seq<i64> param, 3 elements  -> 4201410, same every run
//   get(0) through &Seq<i64> param, w[0]=10  -> a stack address, moves per run
//   sum loop over both                       -> SIGSEGV (rc 139)
//   the same three shapes taking Seq BY VALUE -> 3, 10, 60  (all correct)
//
// Both wrong results are addresses, not data. This file asserts against the
// true length rather than any literal, because one of them is not stable.
//
// The element type is irrelevant: this reproduces on Seq<i64>, so it is not
// the float-classification defect that BLK-20260904 closed. It is why every
// signature migrated in #2413 takes Seq by value.
//
// This file asserts the DEFECT. When the parameter path is fixed this prints 0
// and scripts/ci/language_gap_ratchet_gate.sh fails on purpose.

fn len_via_ref(s: &Seq<i64>) -> i64 with Panic, Epistemic {
    return s.len().unwrap("len")
}

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Panic, Epistemic {
    var w: Seq<i64> = seq_new()
    w.push(10)
    w.push(20)
    w.push(30)

    let got: i64 = len_via_ref(&w)
    if got == 3 {
        // Fixed: the reference now delivers the handle.
        println(0)
    } else {
        // The gap: `got` is address-like garbage, not 3.
        println(1)
    }
    return 0
}
